    "summary_english": "The initial graph is a composite pictograph of water and scattered grains of sand. It originally meant sand, then expanded to sandy ground, desert, fine loose matter, river names, and place names.",
    "etymology": {
      "available": true,
      "headword": "沙",
      "pronunciation": "shā心纽、歌部；生纽、麻韵、所加切。",
      "bibliography": [
        "《金文编》736页",
        "《战文编》745页",
        "《说文》232页",
        "《汉印徵》卷11，9页",
        "《篆隶表》798页"
      ],
      "explanation_original": "初文为合体象形字。像水及散碎沙粒之形。水旁数点本即沙形，恐沙形不显，故从水，似亦兼具会意之义。金文与甲骨文大体同，唯表示沙粒的点数多寡之异。战国文字形体渐趋统一，易象形为会意。\n《说文》：“沙，水散石也。从水，从少，水少沙见。”\n段玉裁注：“石散碎谓之沙。”\n当为后起之义，亦会意之属。本义为沙。后用为沙地﹑沙漠及泛指细碎松散之物，皆引申义。又用为河流名﹑地名等。沙或从“尐”，于义无别。\n（王逸鹤）",
      "images_omitted": true,
      "explanation_english": [
        "The initial graph is a composite pictograph showing water and scattered grains of sand. The dots beside water already represent sand; 水 reinforces the image because the sandy component alone could be unclear, giving the graph an associative element as well. Bronze and oracle-bone forms are broadly alike, differing only in the number of dots for grains. Warring-States writing gradually standardized the graph and transformed the pictograph into an associative construction. 《说文》：“沙，水散石也。从水，从少，水少沙见。” Duan Yucai explains: “石散碎谓之沙。” This is a later explanation and also treats the graph associatively.",
        "The original meaning is sand. Sandy ground, desert, and fine loose material are derived senses, as are river and place names. 沙 can also be written with 尐, without a difference in meaning. （王逸鹤）"
      ]
    },
